course: dictionary
lesson: 2702
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them show which one is meant. «Digestive» is one of these words. In «Pitcher plants have jug-shaped leaves with slippery rims and a pool of digestive liquid», it describes a liquid that breaks down food.
explain: As an adjective, digestive describes a noun, as in digestive system or digestive juices. Look at the nearby words: liquid, juices, system. They are the clues. Digestion is the way the body breaks down food so it can use it.
explain: Digestive can also be a noun. In the UK and Ireland, a digestive is a plain, slightly sweet, round wholemeal biscuit, often dunked in tea. Ask yourself: is this sentence about food inside a body, or a biscuit with a cup of tea? The clues tell you which.
fact: Digestive means to do with digestion, the way the body breaks down food so it can use it.
fact: The digestive system is the set of body parts, from mouth to stomach to intestines, that digests food.
fact: In «After it closes, the flytrap seals tight and makes digestive juices», the plant itself digests its food.
fact: A digestive biscuit is a plain, slightly sweet, round wholemeal biscuit, popular in the UK and Ireland.
fact: Chocolate digestives are very popular, but the word can be a noun or an adjective, so check the clues.
exercise: What does digestive mean in «a pool of digestive liquid»? | To do with rain | A plain, round biscuit | To do with slippery rims | *To do with breaking down food
exercise: In the digestive system, which part comes straight after the stomach? | Oesophagus | *Small intestine | Mouth
exercise: Ellie says, «Can I have a digestive with my tea?» What is a digestive here? | A part of the body | A slippery rim | A liquid from a pitcher plant | *A plain, slightly sweet, round biscuit
test: Which meaning of digestive fits «chocolate digestive»? | To do with digestion | A type of plant | *A biscuit | Slippery and wet
test: «Insects fall in and drown in digestive juice.» What does the digestive juice do to the insect? | *Breaks it down so the plant can use it | Makes it float | Keeps the rain out | Turns it into a biscuit
exam: Jo reads «digestive system», «digestive juices» and «a digestive with tea». Which check helps her most? | Ignore the words around the word | Count the letters in the word | Guess the first meaning she sees | *Swap a meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense
